data.content:
Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`. Here's the confirmed working code:

```csharp
Span<nint>  a = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> b = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

The test ran successfully on .NET, storing and retrieving values (including `nint.MaxValue` and `nuint.MaxValue`) without issue. This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5, when `nint`/`nuint` were introduced as first-class types backed by `System.IntPtr`/`System.UIntPtr`.
